WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:01.789
Right.

00:00:01.789 --> 00:00:04.220
Well mathematicians always talk about beauty
in mathematics but we’ve never been quite

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.650
clear whether it’s the same kind of beauty,
same appreciation of beauty, as people have

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.120
in the arts and humanities.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.910
So, together with my colleagues at University
College London who work in neuroscience, we

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.840
devised an experiment.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.239
And this experiment — which nowadays you
can conduct without cutting people open, you

00:00:21.239 --> 00:00:26.050
have marvelous technology — well to cut
a long story short, this experiment showed

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:32.730
conclusively that the part of the brain that
appreciates mathematical beauty is the same

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.940
part of the brain that appreciates beauty
in other fields.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.240
So scientifically it’s been — and that’s
subject to all sorts of scientific tests done

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.310
with the right technologies and statistics
and so published to the right journals — and

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.700
it showed convincingly that in fact what we
mathematicians describe as beauty or appreciation

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.660
of beauty is fundamentally the same as the
notion of beauty shared by our colleagues

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:54.690
in the arts and humanities.

